WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.020
Take a young kitten.

2
00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.920
Close one eye for six days.

3
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:13.000
Then record from cells in its visual cortex, one at a time.

4
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.560
The closed eye can now drive only a fraction of the cells it drove before.

5
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.700
Now close both eyes instead.

6
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.060
The harm is far smaller.

7
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.280
Why would two closed eyes do less harm than one?

8
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:31.273
Here is how that is measured.

9
00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:37.333
A fine electrode sits next to one cortical cell and records its spikes.

10
00:00:38.373 --> 00:00:41.553
A bar of light is shown to one eye, then the other.

11
00:00:42.613 --> 00:00:45.293
Each cell goes into one of seven groups.

12
00:00:46.313 --> 00:00:48.633
Group 1 answers only to one eye.

13
00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:51.633
Group 7 answers only to the other eye.

14
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:55.213
Group 4 answers about equally to both.

15
00:00:55.893 --> 00:01:01.773
Count the cells in each group, and a normal cat gives a hump, with most cells in the

16
00:01:01.773 --> 00:01:02.773
middle groups.

17
00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:08.287
Now shut one eye of a young kitten for six days.

18
00:01:09.087 --> 00:01:13.587
The closed eye can drive only a fraction of the cells it drove before.

19
00:01:14.547 --> 00:01:17.507
Most of the histogram moves toward the open eye.

20
00:01:18.287 --> 00:01:21.207
We call this a shift in ocular dominance.

21
00:01:22.927 --> 00:01:28.687
A closure from birth for about three months left very few cells that the closed eye

22
00:01:28.687 --> 00:01:31.987
could drive. Yet its retina still responded.

23
00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:36.647
Is this disuse? Try it in kittens.

24
00:01:37.427 --> 00:01:43.587
If unused connections simply wither, closing both eyes should cause a large loss of

25
00:01:43.587 --> 00:01:44.667
responses to both.

26
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:51.927
It did not. With both eyes shut, many cells still answered to each eye, though some

27
00:01:51.927 --> 00:01:53.467
were abnormal or silent.

28
00:01:54.427 --> 00:01:57.247
So the open eye is what hurts the closed one.

29
00:01:57.907 --> 00:02:03.167
The two eyes compete for the same cortex, and the more active eye takes territory

30
00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:04.167
from the other.

31
00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:11.327
Age matters. The same closure in an adult cat, even for months, left the histogram

32
00:02:11.327 --> 00:02:17.007
normal. In kittens, sensitivity began near the start of the fourth week.

33
00:02:17.587 --> 00:02:21.807
It stayed high for several weeks, then declined through the third month.

34
00:02:22.887 --> 00:02:28.567
A window like this, when the brain needs experience, is a critical period or a

35
00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:32.807
sensitive period. Critical periods begin and end abruptly.

36
00:02:33.607 --> 00:02:36.547
Sensitive periods rise and fall gradually.

37
00:02:37.487 --> 00:02:39.287
Real windows often fall between.

38
00:02:40.117 --> 00:02:43.137
This idea helps explain amblyopia.

39
00:02:44.017 --> 00:02:48.957
One eye sees poorly because the two eyes gave the young brain unequal input.

40
00:02:49.997 --> 00:02:51.617
Glasses alone do not fix it.

41
00:02:52.157 --> 00:02:54.057
The treatment follows from competition.

42
00:02:54.957 --> 00:02:58.897
Cover the good eye with a patch, so the weak eye is the more active one.

43
00:02:59.517 --> 00:03:01.197
It works best in young children.

44
00:03:02.117 --> 00:03:06.737
Keep the rule: the more active eye wins, most easily when young.
